# Workflow Rules

## Planning & Communication

- Always plan carefully before making changes. Ask clarifying questions when context is missing or ambiguous.
- Never commit until Bar explicitly approves. Stage changes and present a summary first.
- This project is for **getting production-ready fast** and also for **learning**. Explain decisions when they teach something new.

## Context & Handoff

- At the start of every session, read `docs/HANDOFF.md`, `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, and `docs/RISKS.md`.
- When reaching ~80% of the context window, stop and update `docs/HANDOFF.md` with full context for a new agent session.
- Include: current branch, completed work, pending tasks, blockers, key decisions made, and next steps.

## Git Workflow

- Always work on feature branches. Never commit directly to `main` or `develop`.
- Branch naming: `phase/<name>`, `fix/<name>`, `feat/<name>`.
- Keep commits atomic and well-described.

## Commits

- Informative yet short: one line that says *what* and *why* (max ~72 chars).
- Format: `type: concise description` (e.g. `feat: add celebration overlay on sheet change`).
- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `chore`, `docs`, `style`, `test`.
- Never commit without Bar's explicit approval.

## Pull Requests

- Title mirrors the commit style: `type: what this PR does`.
- Body must include:
  - **Summary** — 1–3 bullets explaining what changed and why.
  - **Test plan** — 3–4 bullet checklist of how to verify (manual or automated).
- Always assign Bar (`Bar-Barina`) as reviewer/assignee.
- Use `gh pr create --assignee Bar-Barina` when creating PRs.

## Spending & Safety

- Never exceed Cursor usage limits. If a task requires many iterations, pause and check with Bar.
- Never make major refactors unless you are certain of the outcome. When in doubt, ask first.
- Prefer small, incremental changes over large rewrites.
